whereas BioMedBERT [32] supports 512 tokens. Accordingly, we designed two strategies: (1) short-context summarization and (2) long-context summarization. For cases in which pathology reports were unavailable, we inserted a placeholder sentence stating 'Text report is not available. '\n\n#### Short-context summarization\n\nSince the MUSK-L encoder is limited to 100 tokens, MUSK adopted a structured prompt consisting of five morphology-related questions [3]. This approach generates a concise structural summary that can serve as model input. To summarize large-scale pathology reports, we followed structured prompts proposed by MUSK. The five categories are as follows: [truncated]","original_chars":2311},{"section_type":"data_representation","heading":"Does CALM generalize to in-house datasets?","heading_level":4,"line_start":207,"line_end":210,"text":"To assess the generalizability of CALM to in-house data, we evaluated it on the KHMC-HNSC cohort. We compared MUSK-based CALM against several baselines, including image-only, text-only, and image and text models. Given the limited sample size and severe class imbalance, we performed zero-shot inference-based evaluation rather than full fine-tuning on the KHMC-HNSC dataset. Specifically, we employed five models pretrained on TCGA-HNSC and tested them directly on the entire KHMCHNSC cohort.","original_chars":493}],"docling_markdown":"data/docling_include_no_vlm_2026-07-09/markdown/full_2026_07_06_rec_003274.md"}]

